<p>&#8220;Philosophical work would be better with adversaries collaborating to keep each other honest.&#8221;<span id="more-58200"></span></p>
<p>Why is that? What would it look like?</p>
<p>In the following guest post, members of the <a href="https://www.mindandmoralitylab.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mind and Morality Lab</a> at Boston University&#8212;<a href="https://www.bu.edu/philo/profile/victor-kumar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Victor Kumar</a> (Boston University), <a href="https://deanderson.weebly.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Derek Anderson</a> (Boston University), <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/emilia-lacy-577a95313/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Emilia Lacy</a> (Boston University), <a href="https://www.joshdmay.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Josh May</a> (University of Alabama, Birmingham), <a href="https://www.suffolk.edu/academics/faculty/m/c/rachel-mckinney" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rachel McKinney</a> (Suffolk University), <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/constantino-themelis-b23105224/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tino Themelis</a> (Boston University), and Velana Valdez (Boston University)&#8212;explain adversarial collaboration and offer answers to those questions.</p>
